{"title":"Wilson Prime","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_Theory\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Number Theory\">Number Theory</a>, a <b>Wilson Prime</b> is a prime number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/a/46aa1fb4a215adc468fe8cb9d2ec17bdb72bf1e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N^2$\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" /> divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/4/024d22c1611fd37d1772e847f55fbaa21e6ea086.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(N-1)!+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />. It bears a striking resemblance to <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Wilson%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Wilson&#39;s Theorem\">Wilson's Theorem</a>. Although conjectured to be infinite in number, no other Wilson primes have been discovered besides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030e19e4e2f1c074127cb50581e8496470c5419f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/d/d5d2ab17089fa7519fe595bdad25e3187bab6dbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$563$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>\\documentclass[12pt]{article}\n\\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb}\n</p><p>\\begin{document}\n</p><p>\\title{\\Large \\textbf{Wilson Primes}}\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\\maketitle\n</p><p>\\large\n</p><p>\\textbf{1. Wilson's Theorem}\n</p><p>Wilson's Theorem states that for any prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/f/6bf121c2ab8ec4c1e864b243b0bee16966de11c4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p - 1)! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p}.\\]\" width=\"191\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Conversely, if this holds, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is prime.\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{2. Motivation}\n</p><p>We now ask a stronger question:\n</p><p>When does the congruence extend to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591f689f950feb4a9cff58ff3166d915f916a405.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" />?\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{3. Wilson Quotient}\n</p><p>Define the Wilson quotient:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/5/19542a7571aeaaccaf9267a364d3d38c8498c6a2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[W_p = \\frac{(p - 1)! + 1}{p}.\\]\" width=\"147\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>Since\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/9/7c9d03dd4bd4a8e6b5379bf3ff0838fa3800b262.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p - 1)! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p},\\]\" width=\"192\" height=\"18\" />\nit follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/a/8ca7445c2df9a2b9cc21da2e6ab8a26fba329db7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"27\" height=\"19\" /> is an integer.\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{4. Definition of Wilson Primes}\n</p><p>A prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is called a Wilson prime if\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/5/0157072ce91f46edeb9e54a2978bf4b8dacdb189.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p - 1)! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p^2}.\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>This is equivalent to:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/2/622fe038040d0f1f3fdbd0bb5aab5eb5d5bec656.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p \\mid W_p.\\]\" width=\"53\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{5. Rewriting the Condition}\n</p><p>We may write:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/d/47d0dff049d07a3a6e94977506995ebcb8dedf79.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p - 1)! = -1 + kp^2\\]\" width=\"153\" height=\"20\" />\nfor some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>This shows the condition is very restrictive.\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{6. Example: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/4/3940bf1382392a6b1b4f106d262cb8e1223d223b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />}\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/d/76dc18143199ceb81bd67813aeba226059c31174.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[4! = 24\\]\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/8/89831cc839c8119bc6d37b3948f61b49ad0ae4b5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[24 + 1 = 25\\]\" width=\"90\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/7/4a706119007e12ef00ead5979ce0672f4c549e47.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[25 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{25}\\]\" width=\"140\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is a Wilson prime.\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{7. Example: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/d/8dd6e9fdf303b4994840a1190b7dabc52cc02e9e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p = 13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"17\" />}\n</p><p>It can be computed that:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/6/4d67829683a4eb167881d40f7f985682e3fb7c47.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[12! + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{13^2}.\\]\" width=\"188\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030e19e4e2f1c074127cb50581e8496470c5419f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is also a Wilson prime.\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{8. Larger Example}\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/3/c132365d65576f4d1dd9f4345d52d310bf1053d9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p = 563\\]\" width=\"61\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>also satisfies:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/5/0157072ce91f46edeb9e54a2978bf4b8dacdb189.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p - 1)! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p^2}.\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{9. Known Wilson Primes}\n</p><p>The only known Wilson primes are:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/8/f18b8656cdd642be23d65b9a3f832be0caf861a9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[5, \\quad 13, \\quad 563.\\]\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{10. Growth of Factorials}\n</p><p>Using Stirling’s approximation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/c/99cffd178881a3a0c3e35a7cf3c61d789ce93541.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p - 1)! \\approx \\sqrt{2\\pi p}\\left(\\frac{p}{e}\\right)^p.\\]\" width=\"181\" height=\"34\" />\n</p><p>This explains why computations become difficult.\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{11. Rarity}\n</p><p>The condition\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/3/ff33c62792f5ba71fe084584eabadc9b62cb7f01.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p - 1)! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p^2}\\]\" width=\"194\" height=\"20\" />\nis extremely rare compared to the usual Wilson theorem.\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{12. Related Concepts}\n</p><p>Similar phenomena appear in:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/2/2d2d29be6efe9d0b75957538cd0cf7ffd80ed598.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Fermat primes}, \\quad \\text{Wieferich primes}.\\]\" width=\"255\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{13. Open Problems}\n</p><p>It is unknown whether:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edfb406155d9909b9eb782ffc8f1d78bd26e778d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{there are infinitely many Wilson primes.}\\]\" width=\"292\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Also unknown:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/f/49fd31deaf1c36e4bbbfc2ef35bfabd8e981bc34.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{whether more exist beyond } 563.\\]\" width=\"233\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>\\vspace{0.3cm}\n</p><p>\\textbf{14. Conclusion}\n</p><p>Wilson primes remain one of the rarest and most mysterious objects in elementary number theory.\n</p><p>\\end{document}\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018113957\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.027 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 103/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5917/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:12015:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018113957 and revision id 280595. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}